[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/id #uuid "244be0b4-aca1-5c1c-abe2-94099e0fbbe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132751" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,2,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,0,1)=\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0,1) & =-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,2,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,2,0)=\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+1-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73207714-b850-5ffd-8c57-6fa10d88caae" :answer-field/id #uuid "73207714-b850-